    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 142300\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n400 PM MST Wed Oct 14 2026\n\n.UPDATE...00Z Aviation Discussion\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry weather is expected over the next couple of days with\n  temperatures warming to near to slightly below normal starting \n  Thursday.\n  \n- Locally windy conditions will impact the western deserts\n  starting Thursday before spreading across the rest of southern\n  and central Arizona Friday into Saturday.\n\n- Wind advisories have been posted for portions of southern\n  Arizona into the Lower Colorado River Valley on Friday with wind\n  gusts in excess of 40 miles per hour expected.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nSkies have cleared after days of clouds and rain and this morning\nwas the coldest since March. Low temperatures this morning were in\nthe 50s across the lower deserts which is around 5-10 degrees\nbelow normal. The temperatures were cooler thanks to a cold front\nassociated with the low pressure system that moved through\nnorthern AZ overnight. This low is no leaving the area and moving\nthrough CO this afternoon. High temperatures this afternoon, \nunder the clear skies, will be in the middle 70s across south-\ncentral AZ lower deserts to around 80 degrees in southwest AZ and\nsoutheast CA.\n\nWith the departure of the upper low and rising H5 heights,\ntemperatures will warm several degrees heading into tomorrow. The\nmorning will still be cool in the 50s to around 60 degrees, thanks\nin part to the clear skies, but afternoon high temperatures will\nwarm back into the middle 80s across the lower deserts. There is\neven a low chance (10-20%) Phoenix reaches 90 degrees. Average\nhigh temperature for mid-October is still around 90 degrees.\n\nWind will become the main focus of the forecast starting tomorrow\nas the pressure gradient across the area strengthens in response\nto strong surface high pressure developing to the north and \nstrong high pressure aloft also spreading into the Great Basin in\nthe wake of the departing upper low. A shortwave trough moving\ndown on the backside of the departing low will actually help\ninduce a weak cutoff low just to the west-southwest of the local\narea by the end of the day tomorrow. Initially tomorrow the \ngreatest surface pressure rises will be to the northeast with a \n1040mb high centered in MT and WY, which will induce breezy \nnortherlies and northeasterlies across the area. Strongest breezes\n(25-35 mph) tomorrow will be focused across northern AZ and \nacross portions of southeast CA, including the lower Colorado \nRiver Valley. The gradient winds will become stronger and the \norientation will shift more E-W heading into Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nStrong easterly winds are expected to develop across southern and\ncentral Arizona by Friday morning with guidance showing H8 winds\nof 30-45 kts across southern and much of central Arizona. Latest\nNAEFS and ENS show H8 winds reaching near record magnitudes \ncompared to climatology. The best corridor for strong winds looks \nto be across southeast Arizona into southern Gila County and \nthrough Pinal, southern Maricopa, and Yuma Counties. Occasional \ngusty winds of 25-40 mph are expected before sunrise in this area \nbefore increasing to advisory level around or just after sunrise \nFriday morning. Wind Advisories have been issued for the \naforementioned areas for Friday morning into the afternoon with \nwind gusts in excess of 40 mph at times. The peak of the winds \nshould occur mid to late morning Friday with some gusts up to\n50-55 mph possible. Areas to the north, including Phoenix, should\nstay below advisory level but gusts of 30-40 mph will be likely \nduring the latter half of the morning Friday. Patchy blowing dust \nwill also be a concern on Friday, particularly across Pinal \nCounty. Winds should eventually drop off some for most areas \nduring the late afternoon hours Friday, but advisory levels winds\nshould continue to be a concern through the rest of Friday and \neven into Saturday across higher terrain areas east and southeast \nof Phoenix.\n\nWinds Saturday will again be elevated with gusts of 25-35 mph for\nmuch of southern and central Arizona, but any advisory level \nwinds are likely to be limited southeast Arizona. Aside from the\nwind concern, guidance is suggesting some lower end shower\npotential later Friday through Saturday. The placement of the\ncut-off low to our southwest starting Friday is expected to advect\na good amount of mid and upper level moisture into the area. Some\nhigh-based light showers may become possible across southern\nArizona as early as Friday afternoon, but little if any\nmeasurable rainfall is expected. These low-end (10-20%) light \nshower chances may stick around through Saturday as the cut-off \nlow begins to drift back through our region, but most locations \nwill struggle to get anything more than a trace to a few \nhundredths of an inch.\n\nEarly next week is likely to be a transition period with weak\nupper level ridging briefly taking over and overall dry\nconditions. After a brief dip in temperatures on Saturday, daytime\nhighs should trend closer to normal for early next week. Looking\nout even further, ensemble guidance is hinting at another deep \nPacific trough moving into the Western U.S. early next week and\npotentially reaching our region during the middle part of next\nweek. This next weather system could bring more rain chances and\nanother cool down during the latter half of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 2300Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\n\nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the TAF period\nunder mostly clear skies. Outside of a slightly northerly tilt to\nthe winds, typical diurnal trends will be favored, with some \nperiods of VRB conditions during the overnight hours. Breezy \nconditions will develop by late Thursday morning, with \nnortheasterly gusts around 20 kt continuing toward the end of the \nforecast window. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo major aviation concerns are expected through tonight as \ngenerally light winds and clear skies prevail. Northerly \nbreeziness will develop during the back half of the TAF period, \nwith gusts around 20-25 kt at each terminal.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDrier air will work into the region over the next couple of days\nleaving mostly clear skies and lowering humidities. MinRHs will\ndrop to 20-25% across the western districts today to 30-45% for \nthe eastern districts before lowering to 20-35% areawide \ntomorrow. Northerly breezy winds will affect southeast CA and \nsouthwest AZ today before increasing to around advisory level \ntomorrow into Friday. The eastern districts will keep overall \nlight winds today before windy conditions settle in Thursday night\ninto Friday. Easterly wind gusts as high as 40-50 mph are expected \nFriday. Some moisture return Friday into Saturday may bring low- \nend rain chances, but wetting rains chances should remain below \n10%.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Wind Advisory from 8 PM to 11 PM MST Thursday for AZZ530.\n\n     Wind Advisory from 5 AM to 3 PM MST Friday for AZZ531-532-536-\n     538-539-554-559.\n\n     Wind Advisory from 5 AM Friday to 11 AM MST Saturday for AZZ552-\n     553-555-558-560>562.\n\n     Wind Advisory from 5 AM to 2 PM MST Friday for AZZ530.\n\nCA...Wind Advisory from 8 PM to 11 PM PDT Thursday for CAZ564-565-569-\n     570.\n\n     Wind Advisory from 5 AM to 2 PM PDT Friday for CAZ564-565-569-\n     570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Benedict\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...RW\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman/Benedict\n",
